**Public list of certifications and seals:** The Board (the European Data Protection Board) must collate all certification mechanisms and data protection seals and marks in a register and make it publicly available by any appropriate means [c2]. Supervisory authorities must also make the certification requirements and criteria public in an easily accessible form and send them to the Board [c1].

**Standards for certifying organizations:** Certification bodies must have an appropriate level of data protection expertise [c3]. They must be accredited by either the competent supervisory authority or the national accreditation body [c3]. The accreditation criteria are drafted and published by the competent supervisory authority [c5], and they are approved by that authority or by the Board [c4]. Where the national accreditation body is used, the bodies must meet EN-ISO/IEC 17065/2012 plus any additional requirements set by the supervisory authority [c3].

To be accredited, a certification body must show that it:
- is independent and has expertise in the subject matter, to the satisfaction of the competent supervisory authority [c6];
- commits to respecting the certification criteria [c6];
- has procedures for issuing, periodically reviewing, and withdrawing certifications, seals, and marks [c6];
- has procedures for handling complaints, which are transparent to data subjects and the public [c6]; and
- can show that its tasks do not create a conflict of interest [c6].
